# SOLID, Modularity, Separation of Concerns

Apply SOLID as package and interface discipline, not class hierarchies. Small packages, one job. Core execution MUST stay independent of CLI (TRUSS constitution).

## Single responsibility

```go
// ❌ Engine prints CLI output
func (e *Engine) Run(ctx context.Context) error {
    fmt.Println("starting")
    return e.exec.Start(ctx)
}

// ✅ Engine returns a result; cmd/ prints
func (e *Engine) Run(ctx context.Context) (Result, error) {
    return e.exec.Start(ctx)
}
```

- `internal/*` must not `fmt` user-facing output. `cmd/` wires flags, I/O, exit codes.
- One package, one purpose. No `internal/util` catch-alls.

## ISP + DIP — small interfaces, accepted where consumed

```go
// ❌ Fat interface next to the implementation
type Worker interface{ Start(context.Context) error; Persist() error }

// ✅ Consumer-owned, minimal
type Starter interface{ Start(context.Context) error }
func NewService(start Starter) *Service { return &Service{start: start} }
```

Interfaces only at real boundaries (multiple impls, tests, I/O). Not "for later."

## Open/closed + substitution

Extend via new types that satisfy a small interface. Honor ctx cancel, wrap errors, never panic on user input.

```go
// ❌ ignored error / no ctx
func (r *Runner) Run(cmd *exec.Cmd) { cmd.Run() }

// ✅
func (r *Runner) Run(ctx context.Context, cmd *exec.Cmd) error {
    if err := run(ctx, cmd); err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("run %s: %w", cmd.Path, err)
    }
    return nil
}
```

Do not add layers, frameworks, or public `pkg/` APIs unless a spec requires them.
